WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.860
Taiwan is a fascinating experiment

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.380
in terms of democratization.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.070
You've got a relatively liberal society.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.020
One way that people
have seen that globally

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.630
is that it's the first place

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.993
in Asia to legalize same-sex marriage.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.250
It's an island of less
than 24 million people,

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.680
and yet its economy is close

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.560
to one of the top 20 in the world today.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.970
But this wealthy, democratic island

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.380
is not considered an
independent country by Beijing.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.180
China has long viewed
Taiwan as its lost territory

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.773
and threatened to unify with it by force.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.010
Under Beijing's pressure,

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.890
only 15 states in the world
recognize Taiwan today,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.920
down from 23 in 2016.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.280
And one key country is not on that list:

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.320
the United States.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.740
The U.S. is Taiwan's biggest ally.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.380
Although there are no
formal diplomatic relations

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.430
between the two.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.440
The U.S. is Taiwan's
biggest arms supplier,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.123
has close economic ties with the island

00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:15.790
and is likely to defend it.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.230
We are going to hold China accountable

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.323
to follow the rules.

00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:23.400
So how has Taiwan become

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.840
the most volatile issue between

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.063
the world's two greatest powers?

00:01:35.379 --> 00:01:37.360
Up to the late 19th century,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.760
Taiwan, then known as Formosa,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.830
passed through the rule of
Dutch and Spanish colonizers,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.540
its aboriginal peoples
and Chinese dynasties.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.050
In 1895, the Qing dynasty
of China lost Taiwan

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.890
to Japan after a
humiliating military defeat.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.490
Taiwan was then ruled by the Japanese

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.760
for the following 50 years.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.430
Japan wanted to make
Taiwan into a model colony,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.980
using it as an example to
persuade other Asian nations

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.090
to join the Japanese empire.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.530
They raised living standards,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.600
they improved education,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.760
they improved health and
built a lot of infrastructure.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.880
So that 50 years later,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.920
Taiwan was actually one

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.783
of the more developed parts of the region.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.600
After World War II,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.600
Taiwan was returned to the control

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.800
of the Chinese government,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.700
at that point, the Republic of China.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.900
The Republic of China rapidly
lost the Chinese Civil War

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.280
to the Communist Party of China,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.430
and as a result ended up on
Taiwan as a regime in exile

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.660
with very few resources,
a lot of refugees,

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:48.970
a very poor population
struggling to survive.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.500
Taiwan relied heavily
on U.S. economic aid,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.000
but by the middle of the 1960s,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.860
most of that aid was phased
out as the U.S. wanted Taiwan

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.010
to develop its own economy.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.815
So Taiwan changed its strategy,

00:03:03.815 --> 00:03:06.210
moving from an import substitution policy,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.510
which replaced foreign
imports with homemade goods,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.070
to an export-oriented economy.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.170
And Taiwan moved very, very quickly

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.540
towards exporting very cheap,

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.040
mostly consumer goods
to the United States.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.100
And as a consequence, over
the next 20 to 30 years,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.773
Taiwan's economy boomed.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.850
So beginning of the 1980s,

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:31.247
the government in Taiwan started to think

00:03:31.247 --> 00:03:33.820
how do we take the next step
in our economic development?

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.970
And they identified among
many areas semiconductors

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.370
as a critical technology.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.690
So that really took off in
the 1990s with the growth

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.430
of Taiwan Semiconductor
Manufacturing Company

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.090
that eventually, you know,

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.410
now today is an absolutely crucial company

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:56.330
for any company that's making technology.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.700
You know, their chips are in smartphones,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.380
they're in tablets, they're in computers,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.780
they're in games consoles
and they're in cars as well.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.610
Anything that you need chips for,

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.480
largely you're having to come to TSMC.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.580
And so this by extension puts Taiwan

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:16.410
in a fairly strong position
that it is a key node

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.150
in the tech supply chain globally.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.290
And so it is a really,
really valuable partner

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.193
for every single country in the world.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.410
Alongside this economic shift,

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.210
Taiwan's political landscape
also changed dramatically.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:34.460
Under the rule of the Chinese
Nationalist Party, or KMT,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.360
for four decades Taiwan
was an authoritarian regime

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:42.110
until the end of martial
law in the late 1980s.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.273
Then Taiwan gradually
transitioned to democracy.

00:04:46.108 --> 00:04:49.990
The first direct presidential
elections took place in 1996,

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.550
followed by several
peaceful transfers of power.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.340
So the Democratic Progressive Party

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.540
is the current party that governs Taiwan.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.990
They were a party that
came out of this movement

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.090
of activists pushing for more democracy

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.390
in Taiwan in the 1970s.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:07.370
And their fundamental stance

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.660
is that Taiwan is already
an independent country.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:11.700
But this isn't

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.993
the same stance held by Beijing.

00:05:25.559 --> 00:05:27.340
So from the People's Republic of China,

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.390
the PRC's perspective,

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.100
Taiwan remains the last piece
of an unfinished civil war.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.840
It's an illegitimate regime.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.780
It's a breakaway territory that needs

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.993
to be unified with the motherland.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.354
The Chiang Kai-shek regime

00:05:42.354 --> 00:05:45.820
won a reprieve in June 1950
when the Korean War broke out.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.690
The United States then
stepped in to prevent

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.080
a final Chinese Communist Party,

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.130
a CCP-led invasion of
the island of Taiwan.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.920
The United States has
been there ever since

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.420
as the guarantor of Taiwan's security.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.830
Despite this guarantee of security,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.560
in 1979, the U.S. broke ties

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.060
with the Republic of China on Taiwan,

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.540
and established them with

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.050
the People's Republic of China in Beijing.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.190
Here things get complicated.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.300
The United States, in
one of our communiques

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.370
with the People's Republic of China,

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:23.370
acknowledged the PRC position
that Taiwan was part of China.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.830
But the United States also did not accept

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.020
the PRC claim to Taiwan.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.040
That has been our position ever since.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.370
And so, in effect,

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:37.370
the U.S. views Taiwan's
status as undetermined.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.220
But even after 1979,

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:43.630
they've maintained informal
relations with Taiwan

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.250
and continue to invest here,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.210
continue to support the economy

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.983
and most importantly, continued
to sell arms to Taiwan.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.990
With the U.S. in the middle,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:00.460
China and Taiwan have avoided
war for over seven decades,

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.430
but tensions between the two sides

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.460
have risen in recent years.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.090
Emerging as a superpower
on the world stage,

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.070
China has become more assertive

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.423
in its claims of sovereignty over Taiwan.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.990
While most of the Taiwanese
prefer maintaining

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.000
the status quo for now,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.110
many young people favor overt
independence for Taiwan.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.930
The young people have
really been at the heart

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.870
of this political transformation
we're seeing in Taiwan

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.290
over the past eight, nine years,

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:31.670
when they started pushing back against

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.970
this economic integration
between China and Taiwan

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.658
that started under former
President Ma Ying-jeou.

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:40.480
One of the best examples was the

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.300
Sunflower Movement that
broke out in March of 2014.

00:07:51.484 --> 00:07:55.200
This movement was led
mostly by college students

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.170
opposed to negotiations with Beijing

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.120
over a free-trade agreement.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.960
The movement occupied
the Legislative Yuan,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.580
Taiwan's parliament,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.360
for more than three weeks in
order to block that parliament

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.370
from considering this trade
agreement and voting on it.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:14.357
So in effect,

00:08:14.357 --> 00:08:18.380
the Sunflower Movement protesters blocked

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.630
further economic integration
with mainland China.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.810
Taiwanese podcasters Kylie Wang

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:27.310
and Ken Young joined some
of the rallies and marches,

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:30.470
and started their popular
news show during the movement.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.073
We have mixed feelings towards China.

00:08:32.073 --> 00:08:33.950
Yeah.
Because on one hand,

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.360
I know that a lot of young
people want to explore

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.660
the big market in China.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:40.880
Because China has just
much more opportunity,

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.010
and sometimes even
better salaries nowadays.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:44.843
Yeah,

00:08:44.843 --> 00:08:47.470
especially for those who
just graduated from college.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.650
On the other hand,

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:50.660
China just keeps on bullying us.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.000
We just don't like what they're doing.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.550
We want to have economic growth,

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:58.930
but we do not want to
sacrifice our economic growth

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:02.360
with our freedom of speech,
with our independence,

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:03.943
with our democracy.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.340
Since the Sunflower Movement,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.960
the China-friendly KMT party has seen

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.580
its polling numbers decline steeply

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.300
while the DPP leader, Tsai Ing-wen,

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.960
won the presidential elections in 2016

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.320
and was re-elected in 2020.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.650
So she has,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.690
as her bottom line effectively

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.680
that Taiwan is an
independent sovereign nation.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.060
Her stance is that we're willing to talk

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.000
but only as equals.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.930
And that is obviously
a position that Beijing

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:35.490
is unwilling to accept.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:37.430
China cut official communications

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:40.120
with Taiwan in 2016

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.140
and stopped individual travel permits

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.370
to the island in 2019.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:48.330
It's also upped the rhetoric
on taking control of Taiwan

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.530
and increased military maneuvers,

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.830
all of which is a concern for the U.S.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.830
Taiwan is by far the
most likely issue to draw

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.880
the United States and China
into a direct military conflict.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:02.360
I should say I don't think
the likelihood of that

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:04.581
is particularly high still,

00:10:04.581 --> 00:10:07.860
but it overshadows all
other potential points

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.690
of friction in the
U.S.-China relationship.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.970
If China and Taiwan did
eventually go to war,

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.130
there could be a devastating cost

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:18.470
to human life and industry.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.400
Even if China manages,

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.810
they could spend years rebuilding Taiwan

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.633
and struggle to integrate
a hostile population.

00:10:26.663 --> 00:10:28.430
So it would be an incredibly dangerous,

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:29.580
messy affair.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:33.960
The attempt to invade a fortified island

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.480
is always going to be an
incredibly difficult one.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.470
So there's a very good reason
they have not attempted

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.240
to take Taiwan by military force.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.640
It's because they're
not sure they can do so.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.790
And so it would be a
really disastrous event

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.190
for everybody involved.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.770
And there's a very good
chance that other democracies

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:53.600
in this region, certainly
Japan, maybe Australia,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:54.950
would get drawn in as well.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.010
Put quite simply,

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:59.650
it's in everybody's
best interest right now

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.200
not to force the issue and to push it off

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.600
for another few years or
maybe even a few decades.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.080
But the unification
would signal to the world

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.400
that it's China, not the U.S.,

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.653
that's the new dominant
power in the region.

00:11:25.367 --> 00:11:28.400
Being able to bring
Taiwan into the PRC fold

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.310
would obviously be a huge achievement

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:31.870
for any Chinese leader.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:35.650
And it's largely seen as
being as one of his ambitions

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:38.653
to achieve before he steps
down, whenever that may be.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.090
Taiwan's future really depends on

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.610
if our allies would take China seriously.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.310
No, I would say it depends on the attitude

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:52.615
of the western countries towards China.

00:11:52.615 --> 00:11:53.448
Yeah.

00:11:53.448 --> 00:11:55.320
Because if the Western
countries, for example,

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.220
the U.S. starts to see Taiwan

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:00.980
as an important geopolitical ally,

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:04.070
then Taiwan could become
a beacon of democracy

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.860
in the Chinese-speaking world.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.870
If not, the western countries would like

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:11.910
to see China growing over
the influence over the world,

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.090
then...

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:14.940
They can just let China be.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:16.374
Yeah,

00:12:16.374 --> 00:12:19.730
and we will be in real risk.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:22.333
Yeah, Taiwan might be in real risk.

00:12:23.297 --> 00:12:25.610
The Trump administration
showed strong support

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.350
for Taiwan by providing more weapons,

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.610
sending high-level officials to visit

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.630
and changing the law to make interactions

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.130
between the two sides much easier.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:38.040
In June, a U.S. delegation visited Taipei

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:41.790
and announced plans to
donate 750,000 doses

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:44.850
of Covid-19 vaccines to help alleviate

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.800
its shortage of shots.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:48.440
Since Biden has taken office,

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.130
the people he's put in
place so far at least

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:55.320
have repeated the Trump
administration's position on Taiwan,

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.610
and have actually taken
quite strong stands in favor

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:00.640
of that approach to Taiwan,

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.570
ignoring or pushing back
against Beijing's objections.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.620
But obviously we'll have
to see over the next four,

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:08.990
potentially eight years to
see how strong that support

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:10.448
for Taiwan actually is.

